1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,420
Productivity can be a struggle for many of us.
对我们许多人来说，保持高效产出可能是一件困难的事。

2
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:11,460
Overflowing email inboxes, housework, social obligations – it can be easy to feel overwhelmed.
爆满的电子邮件收件箱、家务、社会责任——很容易让人感到不堪重负。

3
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:17,930
However, one simple tool that can help us stay on top of everything is the to-do list.
然而，一个能帮助我们掌控一切的简单工具就是待办事项清单。

4
00:00:17,930 --> 00:00:22,900
Write it down, do the tasks and cross them off – simple!
写下来，完成任务，然后划掉——简单！

5
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:31,730
There are three key reasons why lists are beneficial, according to psychologist and author David Cohen.
心理学家兼作家戴维·科恩认为，清单之所以有益，有三个关键原因。

6
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:39,040
Firstly, they help to drown out the chaos and anxiety that comes with having never-ending tasks.
首先，它们有助于压制因永无止境的任务而产生的混乱和焦虑。

7
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:46,060
By writing everything down, we don't have to rely on our memory and can instead focus on the task at hand.
通过把所有事情写下来，我们不必依赖记忆，而是可以专注于手头的任务。

8
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:52,250
Secondly, lists provide structure and guidance, giving us a plan to follow.
其次，清单提供了结构和指导，给了我们一个可以遵循的计划。

9
00:00:52,250 --> 00:01:00,230
As well as this, our brains are more likely to retain information that is presented in a structured and organised manner.
除此之外，我们的大脑更有可能记住以结构化和有组织的方式呈现的信息。

10
00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:08,580
And finally, lists serve as proof of what we have achieved, which can help boost our sense of accomplishment and motivation.
最后，清单可以作为我们已完成事项的证明，这有助于增强我们的成就感和动力。

11
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:15,200
Another possible reason our brains love lists is because of something called the 'Zeigarnik Effect'.
我们的大脑喜爱清单的另一个可能原因是一种叫做"蔡格尼克效应"的现象。

12
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:25,270
This is the name psychologists use for when we remember things we need to do, our unfinished tasks, better than things we have already completed.
心理学家用这个名称来描述这一现象：我们更容易记住需要做的事情，即未完成的任务，而不是已经完成的事情。

13
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:33,040
Researchers from Wake Forest University tested the interference of the Zeigarnik effect on a group of people.
维克森林大学的研究人员测试了蔡格尼克效应对一群人的干扰。

14
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:42,660
The experiment began with a warm-up task, though it was stopped half-way through, and only some people were allowed to make plans to finish it.
实验从一个热身任务开始，但中途被打断，只允许一部分人制定计划来完成它。

15
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:50,900
The researchers found that the group permitted to plan had reduced anxiety and performed better in the second task.
研究人员发现，被允许做计划的那一组人的焦虑感有所降低，并且在第二个任务中表现得更好。

16
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:59,500
The problem was, the others still had the warm-up task stuck in their active memory – an unticked list of tasks.
问题在于，其他人的活跃记忆中仍然卡着那个热身任务——就像一个未勾选的待办事项清单。

17
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:05,630
So, once we tick something off our list, our brain forgets about it and we can relax.
所以，一旦我们从清单上勾掉某项任务，我们的大脑就会忘记它，我们也就可以放松了。

18
00:02:05,630 --> 00:02:11,880
All in all, it seems lists are a valuable tool for staying on top of life!
总而言之，清单似乎是掌控生活的一个宝贵工具！
